Oh, it's worse. INT can go as low as 10 for a wizard. It's highly
unlikely to, but in theory it can. The table's like this (from my local
summarisation of spoilers and sources):

Starting attributes
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
            STR    INT    WIS    DEX    CON    CHA
Minimum      7     10      7      7      7      7
Extra %     10     30     10     20     20     10
Extra avg.   3      9      3      6      6      3
Total avg.  10     19     10     13     13     10
Note that since Wizards can be human or orcish, and those cannot have a
starting INT over 18 and 16 respectively, the above is somewhat skewed
in those cases.

The Extra % means that every extra point distributed has a 30% chance to
end up in INT, _not_ that 30% of points go to INT (it wouldn't be very
random if it did...). Since Wiz's have a lot of extra points to spend,
it's pretty much unheard-of for none of them to go towards INT, but
statistics demands that every one-in-umpty games, it still happens that
way.
(Unless I've got my stats wrong, umpty is in the forty thousands, but
I'm open to corrections by a damned^wstatistics graduate.)

